  "verbalization": "Methazolamide is linked to Slc22a6 through a shared mechanism involving Acetazolamide, a compound that binds to Slc22a6. This relationship is supported by multiple paths that indicate Methazolamide causes various adverse effects, all of which are mediated by Acetazolamide. The lowest common ancestor of Methazolamide and Acetazolamide is the class of compounds known as Thiadiazoles and Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors, suggesting a broader pharmacological context. The consistent binding of Acetazolamide to Slc22a6 across different adverse effects implies a functional role for Slc22a6 in the pharmacological response to these compounds. The integration of these paths highlights a potential mechanism where Slc22a6 is a target of Acetazolamide, which in turn is influenced by Methazolamide, thereby linking the two entities through a shared pathway of action.",
